import{i as e,r as t}from"./scores-PQYd482h.js";var n=[{n:1,unit:`voice`,short:`Hello`,title:`Hello, Music Class`,focus:`Learn how music class works: we sing, we listen, we move, and we stop when the hand goes up.`,outcome:`Students enter a music class, echo a greeting, and freeze on a silent conductor cue.`,materials:[`Open space or chairs in a circle`,`This page on the projector`,`A small drum or clap`],warmup:{title:`Name wave`,detail:`Teacher sings “Hello, _____” on so–mi. The class echoes. Go around as many names as five minutes allow. If time is short, greet the whole class: “Hello, Grade One.”`},teach:[{title:`How we do music`,detail:`In this room we sing, we play, we move, and we listen. We take turns with our voices. We keep our bodies in our own space.`},{title:`The quiet hand`,detail:`When the teacher’s hand goes up, voices and instruments rest. Practice three times: talk, hand up, freeze. Smile when they freeze. Do not shout over them.`},{title:`A hello song`,detail:`Teach “Hello Everybody” (or “Hello, Hello”) by echo. Phrase one, class copies. Phrase two, class copies. Then the whole thing. Keep it high enough for children’s voices — not an adult low voice.`},{title:`Sit like a singer`,detail:`Feet on the floor, tall backs, hands in laps. “If your chair is a drum, we are not playing it yet.”`}],activity:{title:`Echo the teacher`,detail:`Teacher claps a 4-beat pattern. Class claps it back. Then teacher sings a 4-beat so–mi snippet on “loo.” Class sings it back. End with the hello song once more, standing.`},close:`Ask: “What does the quiet hand mean?” Stamp the lesson when most of the class can freeze without a reminder.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Sit in your music spot`,detail:`Find the spot your teacher shows. Hands in your lap. Eyes on the teacher.`},{title:`Sing hello`,detail:`When the teacher sings hello to you, sing hello back with your singing voice.`},{title:`Watch the hand`,detail:`When the hand goes up, your voice stops. That is how we rest together.`},{title:`Echo a clap`,detail:`Copy the teacher’s claps. Same number, same speed.`},{title:`Echo a tune`,detail:`Copy the teacher’s “loo.” Same high and low.`}],teacher:[`Grade 1 is six. Keep instructions to one sentence, then do it.`,`If a child will not sing yet, let them mouth the words. Do not force a solo on week 1.`,`Stand where every child can see your hand. The quiet hand only works if they can see it.`,`Names matter. Misheard names break trust on day one — ask once if you are unsure.`],checklist:[`I know my music spot.`,`I can freeze when the hand goes up.`,`I sang hello with the class.`],mistakes:[`Starting with a long talk. Sing first.`,`Using a low adult key. Children need a higher starting pitch.`,`Praising volume. We want a singing voice, not a shout.`],challenge:`Be the leader: clap a tiny pattern and let the class echo you once.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Draw yourself in music class. Circle the quiet hand.`,items:[{id:`draw`,kind:`text`,label:`I look like this in music class (words or a little picture note):`,lines:3},{id:`hand`,kind:`choice`,label:`When the teacher’s hand goes up I`,choices:[`keep singing`,`freeze and rest`,`get louder`]}]},faith:`Optional: we use our voices as a gift. We take turns so every voice has room.`},{n:2,unit:`voice`,short:`Two voices`,title:`Talking Voice, Singing Voice`,focus:`Feel the difference between a speaking voice and a singing voice, and find a light singing tone.`,outcome:`Students distinguish children’s speaking and singing voices and use a singing voice in echo.`,materials:[`Board: Talk / Sing`,`A picture book sentence`,`This page`],warmup:{title:`Four voices`,detail:`Whisper “good morning,” speak it, call it (playground voice), then sing it on so–mi. Students copy each one. Name them: whisper, talk, call, sing.`},teach:[{title:`Talking sits, singing floats`,detail:`Say “hello” in a talking voice. Now sing “hello” on so–mi. Put a hand on your head for sing, on your chest for talk, so students have a body cue.`},{title:`We do not shout songs`,detail:`A shout hurts ears and does not stay in tune. If the class gets loud, drop to a whisper-sing and they will follow.`},{title:`Match my pitch`,detail:`Sing so on “loo.” Class matches. Then mi. Then so–mi. Smile and nod for close matches. Do not single out a child who is low — sing beside them later.`},{title:`Song: Rain, Rain`,detail:`“Rain, rain, go away” is so–mi. Echo by phrase. Add a gentle finger rain. Keep it light.`}],story:{title:`Mina’s two voices`,body:`Mina used her talking voice to tell Sam about her new boots. Then Mrs. Cole sang “hello.” Mina tried to sing, but it came out like a yell. Mrs. Cole whispered the tune. Mina whispered it back. The whisper grew into a small, floating singing voice. Sam heard it and smiled. Two voices: one for words, one for songs.`},activity:{title:`Sort the voice`,detail:`Teacher says or sings a line. Students show talk (hand on chest) or sing (hand on head). Mix them. End by singing Rain, Rain all the way through.`},close:`Ask two students to demonstrate talk, then sing, on the word “hello.” Stamp when most hands sort correctly.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Copy four voices`,detail:`Whisper, talk, call, sing. Do the one the teacher shows.`},{title:`Hand on chest or head`,detail:`Chest = talking. Head = singing.`},{title:`Match “loo”`,detail:`Sing the same high or low as the teacher. Soft is okay.`},{title:`Sing Rain, Rain`,detail:`Use your singing voice. Finger rain is quiet.`},{title:`Sort talk or sing`,detail:`Listen. Show the right hand sign.`}],teacher:[`Calling voice (playground) is useful to name so they do not use it for songs.`,`If a child only speaks the song, they are still in. Invite, do not force.`,`Pitch-match in a small group during the activity if one child is lost.`],checklist:[`I can show talking voice and singing voice.`,`I did not shout the song.`,`I sang Rain, Rain with the class.`],mistakes:[`Praising the loudest singer.`,`Skipping the body cue. Grade 1 needs something to hold.`],challenge:`Sing your name on so–mi. The class echoes it.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Mark talking or singing.`,items:[{id:`v1`,kind:`choice`,label:`“Please pass the crayons.”`,choices:[`talking`,`singing`]},{id:`v2`,kind:`choice`,label:`“Rain, rain, go away” (the tune)`,choices:[`talking`,`singing`]},{id:`v3`,kind:`choice`,label:`A good music singing voice is`,choices:[`a shout`,`light and floating`,`a whisper only`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:3,unit:`voice`,short:`Echo`,title:`Echo Me`,focus:`Copy short claps and short sung patterns. Echo is how Grade 1 learns almost every song.`,outcome:`Students respond to tone matching and echo games with voice and body.`,materials:[`Drum or lap`,`So–mi ready in your head`,`Space to stand`],warmup:{title:`Copy cat claps`,detail:`Four-beat claps, class echoes. Start easy (ta ta ta ta). Add one rest. Keep a smile. If they rush, slow your model, do not say “you’re rushing.”`},teach:[{title:`Echo means copy`,detail:`I go. You go. Same speed, same number, same tune. We do not add extra claps.`},{title:`Voice echo`,detail:`Sing so–mi on “yoo.” Class echoes. Change the last note sometimes. They have to listen, not guess.`},{title:`Engine, Engine`,detail:`Chant “Engine, engine, number nine” in a speaking voice with a steady beat on laps. Then sing a so–mi version of the first line. Echo by two bars.`},{title:`Alone and together`,detail:`Once the class is strong, let one brave child be the leader for a clap echo. Sitting is fine. No one has to stand in front.`}],activity:{title:`Leader for a turn`,detail:`Circle. Teacher leads three echoes. Then three students each lead one clap pattern. Class copies. Finish with Engine, Engine all together.`},close:`Ask: “Does an echo add extra sounds?” No. Stamp when echoes stay the same length as the model.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Wait for the whole pattern`,detail:`Do not start copying until the teacher is done.`},{title:`Copy claps`,detail:`Same number. Same speed.`},{title:`Copy the sung “yoo”`,detail:`Same high and low.`},{title:`Chant Engine, Engine`,detail:`Pat the beat on your legs while you say the words.`},{title:`Lead one clap if you want`,detail:`Four beats. The class will copy you.`}],teacher:[`Wait. The pause before they echo is where listening lives.`,`If they start early, freeze, smile, try again. Do not scold.`,`Keep sung echoes in so–mi this week. La can wait.`],checklist:[`I waited until the teacher finished.`,`My echo was the same length.`,`I chanted Engine, Engine with the beat.`],mistakes:[`Patterns that are too long. Four beats is enough.`,`Talking through their echo. Model, then silence.`],challenge:`Sing an echo on “yoo” that goes so–mi–so–mi. Can a friend copy it?`,worksheet:{prompt:`What is an echo?`,items:[{id:`echo`,kind:`choice`,label:`An echo is`,choices:[`copy the teacher`,`make up a new song`,`get louder`]},{id:`wait`,kind:`choice`,label:`I start my echo`,choices:[`in the middle`,`when the teacher is done`,`whenever I want`]}]}},{n:4,unit:`voice`,short:`So–mi`,title:`So and Mi`,focus:`Two pitches: so (higher) and mi (lower). Hand signs and a tiny song.`,outcome:`Students respond to so–mi hand signals and sing so–mi songs in tune.`,materials:[`Space for hands`,`See-Saw or Rain, Rain`,`Board: so (high) / mi (low)`],warmup:{title:`Elevator`,detail:`Hands low: hum a low mi. Hands high: hum a higher so. Ride the elevator three times. No words yet.`},teach:[{title:`So is higher, mi is lower`,detail:`Show Kodály so (palm forward at forehead) and mi (palm in at chin). Students copy the signs without sound, then with “so” and “mi.”`},{title:`I sing, you sign`,detail:`Teacher sings so or mi. Students only show the hand sign. Then reverse: teacher signs, students sing.`},{title:`See-Saw`,detail:`“See-saw, up and down” sits on so–mi. Bounce a gentle knee bend on the beat. Echo, then all.`},{title:`Do not stretch so too high`,detail:`If so is a strain, drop the whole song a bit. Grade 1 should not look tight in the neck.`}],activity:{title:`Sign the song`,detail:`Sing See-Saw. Students show so and mi while they sing. Then a few students take turns being the “hand teacher” while the class sings.`},close:`Silent hand signs: class sings what they see. Two patterns. Stamp when most signs match the sound.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Show so`,detail:`Hand up near your forehead. That is the higher one.`},{title:`Show mi`,detail:`Hand a little lower. That is the lower one.`},{title:`Sing what the hands say`,detail:`Watch the teacher’s hands. Sing so or mi.`},{title:`Sing See-Saw`,detail:`Bend your knees a little on the beat. Use a singing voice.`},{title:`Sign while you sing`,detail:`Hands and voice do the same job.`}],teacher:[`Hand signs stay in front of the body, not wildly over the head.`,`If a child reverses so and mi, stand beside them and do it together. Do not quiz them in front of the class.`,`See-Saw, Rain Rain, and Star Light are enough repertoire this week.`],checklist:[`I can show so and mi with my hands.`,`I know so is higher.`,`I sang See-Saw with the class.`],mistakes:[`Adding la this week. Two pitches is the win.`,`Tiny hand signs no one can see. Make them clear.`],challenge:`Teacher points to two friends: they sing a so–mi conversation (“Hi.” “Hi.”).`,worksheet:{prompt:`Circle the higher one.`,items:[{id:`high`,kind:`choice`,label:`Which is higher?`,choices:[`so`,`mi`]},{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`A so–mi song we sang today:`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:5,unit:`voice`,short:`Games`,title:`Singing Games`,focus:`A song can be a game. We sing, we move, we take turns, we keep the tune.`,outcome:`Students sing in tune many rhythmic and melodic songs, singing games and action songs.`,materials:[`Circle space`,`One singing game (Lucy Locket or Bounce High)`,`Beanbag or small ball optional`],warmup:{title:`Bounce High, Bounce Low`,detail:`Seated bounce on the beat, then standing. Words: “Bounce high, bounce low, bounce the ball to Shiloh.” Swap in a class name. So–mi–la sneaks in; do not lecture it yet.`},teach:[{title:`A singing game has a job`,detail:`We sing the whole time. The game is not a recess shout. If the tune falls apart, freeze, sing once sitting, then play again.`},{title:`Lucy Locket`,detail:`Circle. One child walks the beat on the outside with a “pocket” (beanbag). At the end of the verse they drop it behind a friend. That friend walks next. Keep the singing going.`},{title:`Kind turns`,detail:`Everyone gets a turn over the year, not always today. We do not grab. We do not say “you missed.”`},{title:`Still a singing voice`,detail:`Excitement makes shouts. Remind: light voice, strong beat in the feet.`}],activity:{title:`Play the game`,detail:`Run Lucy Locket or Bounce High for the middle of the block. Sit after two or three turns to reset the tune, then play again.`},close:`Sit. Sing the song once with no game. That is how we remember it. Stamp if the tune survived the game.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Stand in a circle`,detail:`Hands to yourself. Eyes can follow the walker.`},{title:`Sing the whole time`,detail:`The game does not pause the song.`},{title:`Walk on the beat if you are the walker`,detail:`Not a run. Match the song.`},{title:`Kind hands`,detail:`No grabbing the beanbag. No pushing.`},{title:`Sit and sing it once more`,detail:`No game. Just the tune.`}],teacher:[`Pick walkers who need a win, not only the confident kids.`,`If the class cannot hold the tune and the game, drop the game and just sing this week. Try the game again in week 6.`,`Beanbags on the floor behind a child, not at their head.`],checklist:[`I sang during the game.`,`I kept my hands kind.`,`I can still sing the song sitting down.`],mistakes:[`Letting the game become tag. Freeze and reset.`,`Too many rules before the first play. Show once, play.`],challenge:`A pair sings Bounce High as a duet while the class pats the beat.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our singing game.`,items:[{id:`game`,kind:`text`,label:`The game we played:`,lines:1},{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`During a singing game I should`,choices:[`only run`,`sing the whole time`,`talk to my neighbour`]}]}},{n:6,unit:`voice`,short:`Together`,title:`We Can Sing Together`,focus:`Unison: one tune, many voices. This is hand-in project 1 — our class song.`,outcome:`Students sing accurately in unison and respond to a conductor’s start and stop.`,materials:[`The class song (See-Saw, Rain Rain, or Hello)`,`Quiet hand ready`,`Optional: record a take for families`],warmup:{title:`Start and stop`,detail:`Breathe together. Hand in: sing “loo” on so. Hand out: rest. Four tries. This is conducting for six-year-olds.`},teach:[{title:`Unison means one tune`,detail:`Not faster, not louder than your neighbour. Match. If you cannot hear the person beside you, you are too loud.`},{title:`Polish the class song`,detail:`Words, then tune, then both. Fix one muddy phrase by echo. Do not restart the whole song ten times — fix the sticky bar.`},{title:`Standing tall`,detail:`Feet quiet, faces toward the conductor. Hands still unless the song has actions.`},{title:`One sharing take`,detail:`Sing it through as if someone is listening. Celebrate the stop as much as the start.`}],activity:{title:`Project 1 — class song`,detail:`Two practice runs, one “sharing” run. Optional: record on a classroom device to send home or play later. No solos required.`},close:`Name one thing the class did well (starts, soft voices, still feet). Stamp project 1 when the sharing run holds together.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Watch for the start`,detail:`Breathe when the teacher breathes. Sing on the hand.`},{title:`Match your neighbour`,detail:`Same tune. Same speed. Not a race.`},{title:`Use a singing voice`,detail:`Light. Not a shout.`},{title:`Stop together`,detail:`When the hand cuts off, the sound ends.`},{title:`Stand tall for the sharing run`,detail:`This is our class song.`}],teacher:[`Rubric is tiny: singing voice, start/stop, words. Do not grade pitch perfectly in week 6.`,`A child who mouths the words still belongs in the video.`,`If you record, check district photo/video rules first.`],checklist:[`I watched the teacher for the start.`,`I used a singing voice.`,`I stopped when the hand said stop.`],mistakes:[`Restarting for one cough. Keep going.`,`Picking a brand-new song today. Use one they know.`],challenge:`Add one action that matches a word, and keep the tune.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our class song.`,items:[{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`The song we shared:`,lines:1},{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`Unison means`,choices:[`everyone sings the same tune`,`everyone sings a different tune`,`only the teacher sings`]}]}},{n:7,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Feel the beat`,title:`Feel the Beat`,focus:`The beat is the steady heart of the song — even when the words are long or short.`,outcome:`Students understand that music may move to a steady beat and show it on their body.`,materials:[`Heart drawn on the board`,`A drum`,`A known song`],warmup:{title:`Heartbeat`,detail:`Hand on chest. Feel your own heart if you can. Teacher plays a slow steady drum. Students pat that same speed on their legs. “The drum is the heart of the music.”`},teach:[{title:`Beat does not stop for big words`,detail:`Say “cat, cat, cat, cat” on the beat. Then “elephant, cat, elephant, cat.” The lap pats stay even. The words change. That even pat is the beat.`},{title:`Show it three ways`,detail:`Pat. Step in place. Quiet two-finger clap. Switch on your cue so they do not get stuck in one motion.`},{title:`Song on top`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain or Hello while they pat. If pats follow the words instead of the beat, go back to drum only.`},{title:`The beat can be slow or faster`,detail:`Two tempos only today. Still even. Not “as fast as you can.”`}],activity:{title:`Drum is the boss`,detail:`Teacher drum leads. Students match. Fade the drum and see if their pats stay even. Bring the drum back if they rush.`},close:`Ask a student to play four even beats on the drum. Class pats with them. Stamp if most pats stay even without the words pulling them.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Pat the drum’s speed`,detail:`Even pats. Not the words yet.`},{title:`Keep patting on long words`,detail:`Elephant still gets even pats.`},{title:`Try feet in place`,detail:`Soft shoes. Same speed as your hands.`},{title:`Sing and pat`,detail:`Song in your voice, beat in your body.`},{title:`Notice slow and faster`,detail:`Both are still a beat if they stay even.`}],teacher:[`Do not introduce ta yet. This week is only the even heart.`,`Sitting pats are easier than walking. Save walking for week 8.`,`A child who pats twice as fast is rushing — model beside them.`],checklist:[`I can pat a steady beat.`,`My pats stay even on long words.`,`I sang and patted at the same time.`],mistakes:[`Letting them pound. Quiet body percussion.`,`Talking about notes and stems. Not yet.`],challenge:`Pat the beat with eyes closed for eight beats. Did you stay with the drum?`,worksheet:{prompt:`The beat is the even heart.`,items:[{id:`beat`,kind:`choice`,label:`The beat is`,choices:[`even, like a heart`,`only the long words`,`as fast as I can go`]},{id:`show`,kind:`text`,label:`I can show the beat with my`,lines:1}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:8,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Walk it`,title:`Walk the Beat`,focus:`Take the steady beat into the feet. Walk, freeze, walk again.`,outcome:`Students move to the beat through walking, as appropriate to their psychomotor development.`,materials:[`Clear floor`,`Drum or song`,`Tape spots optional`],warmup:{title:`Seat beat, then stand`,detail:`Eight pats sitting. Stand. Eight steps in place. Sit. Repeat. Check shoes and space.`},teach:[{title:`Walk the drum`,detail:`Teacher plays even beats. Students walk the room on those beats. Not a race. Soft knees. Own space.`},{title:`Freeze is part of music`,detail:`Drum stops, feet stop. Practice five times. Celebrate still statues.`},{title:`The song walks too`,detail:`Sing a known song while walking the beat. If they skip to the words, go back to drum only.`},{title:`Directions`,detail:`Walk forward. Walk in a circle. Walk back to your spot by the last phrase. Give the “home” cue early.`}],activity:{title:`Walk and freeze`,detail:`Three songs or drum grooves. Freeze on silence. End by walking home to spots and sitting on the last beat.`},close:`Sitting: “Did your feet follow the drum or the words?” Stamp if freezes are clean.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Find a safe space`,detail:`Arms not touching friends.`},{title:`Walk on the drum`,detail:`One step for each beat. Soft feet.`},{title:`Freeze when it stops`,detail:`Statue. No extra steps.`},{title:`Walk while we sing`,detail:`Feet stay even.`},{title:`Walk home on time`,detail:`Be on your spot by the last beat.`}],teacher:[`Gallop and skip wait until bodies are ready — many Grade 1s still need a walk.`,`If the room is tiny, walk in place or in a circle only.`,`A child who cannot walk the beat can pat on a chair and still be in.`],checklist:[`I walked on the beat.`,`I froze when the drum stopped.`,`I kept my body in my own space.`],mistakes:[`Turning it into tag.`,`A tempo they cannot walk. Slow is kind.`],challenge:`Be a quiet leader: four students match your walk without a drum, only your feet.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Feet on the beat.`,items:[{id:`freeze`,kind:`choice`,label:`When the drum stops I`,choices:[`keep walking`,`freeze`,`run`]},{id:`feel`,kind:`text`,label:`Walking the beat felt`,lines:1}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:9,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Hands and freeze`,title:`Hands, Feet, and Freeze`,focus:`Switch the beat between body parts. Control is the skill.`,outcome:`Students accompany singing with body percussion on the beat and freeze on cue.`,materials:[`Known song`,`Cue cards: hands / feet / freeze`,`Drum`],warmup:{title:`Simon beat`,detail:`“Beat in your hands.” “Beat in your feet.” “Beat on your shoulders.” Change every eight beats. Freeze on “rest.”`},teach:[{title:`The beat can move around the body`,detail:`Same even heart, new place. This is body percussion without fancy names yet.`},{title:`Shoulders and heads are gentle`,detail:`No hitting. Two fingers. Model the volume you want.`},{title:`Sing on top`,detail:`See-Saw or Engine, Engine. Teacher points to hands or feet. Class keeps the song going while the beat location changes.`},{title:`Freeze is a rest for the body`,detail:`Connect to next unit’s silence, but do not name ta yet.`}],activity:{title:`Cue-card beat`,detail:`A student helper holds HANDS or FEET. Teacher still controls freeze. Rotate the helper.`},close:`Name a child who changed on the first try. Stamp if the song survived the switches.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Watch the cue`,detail:`Hands, feet, or freeze.`},{title:`Keep the beat even`,detail:`A new body part is not a faster beat.`},{title:`Gentle hands`,detail:`We do not hurt our bodies to make a beat.`},{title:`Keep singing`,detail:`The song does not stop when the beat moves.`},{title:`Helper job`,detail:`If you hold a card, show it clearly and still sing.`}],teacher:[`Helpers who struggle with beat can still hold the card — that is a real job.`,`If noise spikes, sit and do only hands.`],checklist:[`I can put the beat in my hands or feet.`,`I freeze on cue.`,`I keep singing.`],mistakes:[`Too many body parts. Hands, feet, freeze is enough.`],challenge:`Switch every four beats instead of eight. Still even?`,worksheet:{prompt:`Where was the beat?`,items:[{id:`where`,kind:`text`,label:`Today the beat lived in my`,lines:1},{id:`gentle`,kind:`choice`,label:`Body percussion should be`,choices:[`gentle`,`as hard as I can`,`only on a friend`]}]}},{n:10,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Strong beats`,title:`Strong Beats and Quiet Beats`,focus:`Some beats feel heavier. We can show a strong beat without getting loud and wild.`,outcome:`Students understand that there are strong and weak beats in music.`,materials:[`Drum`,`A 2-beat song (See-Saw) and a 4-beat chant`,`Board: STRONG quiet STRONG quiet`],warmup:{title:`March in place`,detail:`Left is stronger, right is lighter. “STRONG, quiet, STRONG, quiet.” Keep it small so knees stay safe.`},teach:[{title:`Strong is not shout`,detail:`A strong beat is a little heavier, not angry. Model a tiny knee bend on 1.`},{title:`Groups of 2`,detail:`See-Saw. Bend on “See,” light on “saw.” Pat-clap. Pat is strong.`},{title:`Groups of 4`,detail:`Engine, Engine. Strong on “En-gine” downbeats you choose — keep it simple: beat 1 is stronger, 2 3 4 stay light.`},{title:`Conducting`,detail:`Teacher’s hand is bigger on the strong beat. Students’ bodies match.`}],activity:{title:`Statue on 1`,detail:`Walk the beat. On each strong beat, a tiny freeze-frame, then keep going. Silly but controlled. Then sit and pat-clap a known song.`},close:`Ask: “Is the strong beat the yelling beat?” No. Stamp if strong/weak is visible in their bodies.`,studentSteps:[{title:`March in place`,detail:`One foot feels heavier.`},{title:`Pat-clap`,detail:`Pat = strong. Clap = quiet.`},{title:`Sing See-Saw with a bend on See`,detail:`Small bend.`},{title:`Watch the big hand`,detail:`That is beat one.`},{title:`Keep the quiet beats alive`,detail:`Do not skip them.`}],teacher:[`Do not say 2/4 and 4/4. Say groups of two and groups of four.`,`If they stomp, take shoes off the stomp and go back to finger pats.`],checklist:[`I can show a strong beat and a quiet beat.`,`I did not yell on the strong beat.`,`I kept the quiet beats going.`],mistakes:[`Only playing the strong beats. Weak beats matter.`],challenge:`Conduct the class with a big hand on 1 and small hands on the rest.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Strong and quiet.`,items:[{id:`strong`,kind:`choice`,label:`A strong beat should be`,choices:[`a little heavier`,`a shout`,`skipped`]},{id:`group`,kind:`choice`,label:`See-Saw feels like groups of`,choices:[`two`,`twenty`,`one long shout`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:11,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Beat vs words`,title:`Beat vs the Words`,focus:`The beat stays even. The rhythm is the pattern of the words. They are not the same.`,outcome:`Students begin to separate a steady beat from the rhythm of the words.`,materials:[`Board with two lines: beat (even dots) and words (long/short)`,`Known chants`],warmup:{title:`Two jobs`,detail:`Half the class pats even beats. Half speaks “Engine, engine, number nine.” Swap.`},teach:[{title:`Give them names`,detail:`Beat = even heart. Rhythm = the way the words go. We will clap the words next week as ta and ti-ti. Today we only feel the difference.`},{title:`Draw it`,detail:`Four even dots on the board. Under them, write Engine engine number nine and swoop the syllables. Dots stay even. Syllables bunch.`},{title:`Hands vs laps`,detail:`Laps keep the beat. Hands tap the words. This is hard. Do it slowly. Many will mix them — that is the lesson, not a failure.`},{title:`Song check`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain. Beat on laps. Then tap the words on shoulders. Ask which one stayed even.`}],activity:{title:`Split the class`,detail:`Line A: beat. Line B: words. Conduct them in and out. Swap. End all together on beat only, to land on something they can succeed at.`},close:`“Which one is even?” Beat. Stamp if they can point to it.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Pat four even beats`,detail:`Heart.`},{title:`Speak the chant`,detail:`Words have their own pattern.`},{title:`Watch the board`,detail:`Dots stay even. Words do not.`},{title:`Try two jobs with a partner`,detail:`One beat, one words. Then swap.`},{title:`Finish on the beat`,detail:`Everyone pats together.`}],teacher:[`This is the conceptual leap of Grade 1 rhythm. Do not rush to notation if they are wobbly.`,`Partners help. Choose them.`],checklist:[`I know the beat is even.`,`I know the words can be long and short.`,`I tried one job while a friend did the other.`],mistakes:[`Using the word “rhythm” as a scold (“keep the rhythm”). Today it has a precise meaning.`],challenge:`Tap the words to your name. Does your name fit in four beats?`,worksheet:{prompt:`Beat or words?`,items:[{id:`even`,kind:`choice`,label:`The even heart is the`,choices:[`beat`,`words`]},{id:`name`,kind:`text`,label:`Clap the way your name goes. Write your name:`,lines:1}]}},{n:12,unit:`rhythm`,short:`Parade`,title:`Beat Parade`,focus:`Hand-in project 2: walk or play a steady beat, freeze on silence, stay safe.`,outcome:`Students maintain a steady beat with body or instrument and freeze on rest.`,materials:[`Classroom percussion for half the class if you have it`,`Parade path`,`Known song`],warmup:{title:`Silent parade`,detail:`Walk the beat with no talking. Drum only. Freeze. Sit.`},teach:[{title:`What “done” looks like`,detail:`Even feet or even instrument. Freeze on stop. Instruments stay quiet until the start hand.`},{title:`If you have a shaker or sticks`,detail:`Play the beat, not the words. Two-finger volume. Sticks are not swords.`},{title:`The song is the street`,detail:`Teacher sings. Parade moves. At the fermata or drum stop, statues.`},{title:`Audience spots`,detail:`Half parade, half watch. Watchers check: even? safe? freeze? Swap.`}],activity:{title:`Project 2 — parade`,detail:`Two rehearsal parades, one sharing parade. Watchers give one kind comment: “I saw even feet.”`},close:`Collect instruments carefully — that is still music class. Stamp project 2.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Wait for the start`,detail:`Instrument down until the hand.`},{title:`Play or walk the beat`,detail:`Even. Not the words.`},{title:`Freeze on stop`,detail:`No extra shake.`},{title:`Safe tools`,detail:`Sticks down when you walk to your seat.`},{title:`Watchers watch for even feet`,detail:`Say one kind thing.`}],teacher:[`Not enough instruments? Body percussion is a full project.`,`Rubric: even beat, freeze, safety. That is all.`],checklist:[`My beat stayed even.`,`I froze on the stop.`,`I used my instrument or body with care.`],mistakes:[`A long winding parade in a crowded room. A circle is enough.`],challenge:`Play the beat on a drum while the class walks. You are the heart.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Beat parade.`,items:[{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`In the parade I`,choices:[`kept an even beat`,`played as fast as I could`,`chased a friend`]},{id:`proud`,kind:`text`,label:`I am proud that I`,lines:2}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:13,unit:`melody`,short:`Long short`,title:`Long and Short`,focus:`Sounds can be long or short. We hear it, we draw it, we clap it.`,outcome:`Students understand that music is made up of long sounds, short sounds and silences.`,materials:[`Board`,`Yarn or drawn lines (long dash, two short dashes)`,`Voices`],warmup:{title:`Stretch and tap`,detail:`Slide a long “oooo” with arms opening. Then two short “ti ti” finger taps. Alternate.`},teach:[{title:`Long takes more time`,detail:`Hold “luuuu.” Short is “ti-ti.” Both can sit on a beat: one long fills a beat, two shorts share a beat. Say it in kid words: one long sound for one step, two quick sounds for one step.`},{title:`Draw icons`,detail:`A long macaroni. Two little dots. No stems required. Grade 1 can write these.`},{title:`Names come next week`,detail:`Today: long and short. Tomorrow: ta and ti-ti. Let the ears go first.`},{title:`Song hunt`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain. Which words feel longer? “Rain” can be long. “Go a-way” has shorter bits. Do not over-analyse.`}],activity:{title:`Compose with yarn`,detail:`Pairs get four beats of space on the floor or paper. They lay one long or two shorts in each beat. Clap it. Switch with a neighbour.`},close:`Class claps one pair’s pattern. Stamp if they can tell long from short by ear.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Make a long sound`,detail:`Stretch it. Do not shout it.`},{title:`Make two short sounds`,detail:`They share one step.`},{title:`Draw long and short`,detail:`Macaroni and two dots.`},{title:`Build four beats`,detail:`With a partner. Then clap it.`},{title:`Listen to another pair`,detail:`Copy their pattern.`}],teacher:[`Keep silences for week 16. Too many ideas at once will blur.`,`If you use real notation, still say long and short out loud.`],checklist:[`I can make a long sound and short sounds.`,`I can draw them.`,`I clapped a four-beat idea.`],mistakes:[`Calling everything “notes.” Use long and short.`],challenge:`Sing your four-beat picture on so–mi.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Draw four beats. Use long lines and short-short pairs.`,items:[{id:`draw`,kind:`text`,label:`My four beats (describe or list long/short):`,lines:3},{id:`ear`,kind:`choice`,label:`Two short sounds in one beat are`,choices:[`faster little sounds`,`two long sounds`,`a freeze`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:14,unit:`melody`,short:`Ta`,title:`Ta`,focus:`The long sound that fills one beat has a name: ta. We say it, clap it, and see it.`,outcome:`Students use ta (quarter note) to show one sound on one beat.`,materials:[`Icon or stemless ta on the board (stick or apple)`,`Known 4-beat chant`],warmup:{title:`Four tas`,detail:`Say “ta ta ta ta” while stepping. Clap it. Whisper it. That is the whole warmup.`},teach:[{title:`Ta is one sound on one beat`,detail:`Point to four tas on the board. Touch each as you say it. Students write four tas in the air.`},{title:`Icon they can draw`,detail:`A simple stick, a heart, or a stemless note. Pick one and stay with it all year.`},{title:`Find ta in a chant`,detail:`“One, two, tie my shoe” — many tas. Pat and say ta instead of words, then put words back.`},{title:`Read a line`,detail:`Board: ta ta ta ta. Class reads. Change beat 4 later in the block when rest arrives in week 16 — not yet.`}],activity:{title:`Write four tas`,detail:`On the worksheet or mini whiteboards, four tas. Walk and read a friend’s. Play them on laps.`},close:`One child points, class says ta. Stamp if the pointing matches the speaking.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Say ta four times`,detail:`Even. With your feet if you want.`},{title:`Draw ta the class way`,detail:`Copy the board.`},{title:`Read the board`,detail:`Point and say.`},{title:`Put ta under a chant`,detail:`Words, then tas, then words.`},{title:`Read a friend’s tas`,detail:`Four beats.`}],teacher:[`Do not introduce the quarter-note name unless you love it. Ta is enough in Grade 1.`,`Keep reading left to right. Some children will poke at random.`],checklist:[`I can say ta.`,`I can draw ta.`,`I can read four tas.`],mistakes:[`Writing eight tiny tas in one beat. One ta, one beat.`],challenge:`Conduct four tas with a straight finger, left to right.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Write four tas. Point as you say them.`,items:[{id:`tas`,kind:`text`,label:`My four tas:`,lines:2},{id:`mean`,kind:`choice`,label:`Ta means`,choices:[`one sound on one beat`,`two sounds on one beat`,`no sound`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:15,unit:`melody`,short:`Ti-ti`,title:`Ti-ti`,focus:`Two short sounds that share one beat: ti-ti.`,outcome:`Students use ti-ti (two eighth notes) as two sounds on one beat.`,materials:[`Board: ta vs ti-ti`,`Chants with pairs of shorts (Pease Porridge Hot)`],warmup:{title:`Slow ta, quick ti-ti`,detail:`Step ta. Jog in place ti-ti on the same beat speed — two little steps in the time of one. Hold a chair if jogging is too much; two finger taps instead.`},teach:[{title:`Ti-ti shares the beat`,detail:`One ta = one sandwich. Ti-ti = two halves. They take the same plate (the beat).`},{title:`Draw two little notes together`,detail:`Two sticks with a beam, or two dots with a line. Stay consistent with week 14’s icon style.`},{title:`Pease Porridge Hot`,detail:`Clap the words. Find the quick pairs. Say them as ti-ti. Keep the beat in the feet.`},{title:`Read mixed lines`,detail:`ta ta ti-ti ta. Point. If they say ti-ti too slowly, it becomes two tas — fix it kindly with a model.`}],activity:{title:`Build a bar`,detail:`Four beat boxes on paper. Fill each with ta or ti-ti. Clap. Trade papers.`},close:`Ear quiz: teacher claps ta or ti-ti. Students hold one finger or two. Stamp if most are right.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Clap ti-ti`,detail:`Two quick claps in one beat.`},{title:`Draw ti-ti`,detail:`Two friends in one box.`},{title:`Read ta ta ti-ti ta`,detail:`Point as you go.`},{title:`Find ti-ti in a chant`,detail:`The quick words.`},{title:`Fill four boxes`,detail:`Ta or ti-ti in each.`}],teacher:[`The error will be ti-ti as two full beats. Use the sandwich plate picture again.`,`No rest yet unless they ask. “Empty box” is next week.`],checklist:[`I can clap ti-ti in one beat.`,`I can read a line with ta and ti-ti.`,`I can hear the difference.`],mistakes:[`Writing ti-ti as two tas in two boxes.`],challenge:`Make a pattern that uses both ta and ti-ti and teach it to your row.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Fill four beats with ta or ti-ti.`,items:[{id:`line`,kind:`text`,label:`My pattern (write ta and ti-ti):`,lines:2},{id:`share`,kind:`choice`,label:`Ti-ti means`,choices:[`two sounds in one beat`,`one long sound`,`stop`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:16,unit:`melody`,short:`Rest`,title:`Sh — the Rest`,focus:`Silence is music too. A rest is one beat of no sound, with a still body.`,outcome:`Students show silences as a one-beat rest (sh) inside a four-beat pattern.`,materials:[`Board: rest icon (z or a sh finger)`,`Drum`],warmup:{title:`Play, rest, play, rest`,detail:`Clap, open hands (silence), clap, open hands. The open hands are the rest. Faces stay ready — rest is not slump.`},teach:[{title:`Rest is a beat with no sound`,detail:`We still feel it. We do not skip it. We do not say “sh” loudly if that becomes a sound — a finger on lips or open palms works.`},{title:`Write it`,detail:`A squiggle, a Z, or the traditional rest. Pick one.`},{title:`Read ta ta rest ta`,detail:`The tricky part is staying still for the full beat. Count it in your head.`},{title:`Put a rest in a known chant`,detail:`Make beat 4 silent in Engine, Engine. Giggle once, then make the silence real.`}],activity:{title:`Mystery rest`,detail:`Teacher claps a four-beat pattern with one rest. Students point to which beat was silent (1, 2, 3, or 4). Then they write their own with one rest and perform.`},close:`Class statue for one full rest. Stamp if rests keep their full beat.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Show a silent beat`,detail:`Open hands. No extra clap.`},{title:`Read ta ta sh ta`,detail:`The sh is a full beat.`},{title:`Write one rest in four beats`,detail:`Then clap it.`},{title:`Guess the silent beat`,detail:`Listen to the teacher.`},{title:`Keep your body ready`,detail:`Rest is not falling asleep.`}],teacher:[`If they giggle through rests, make a game: who can keep the quietest rest?`,`Do not combine this with new pitches today.`],checklist:[`I can show a rest with no sound.`,`My rest lasts a whole beat.`,`I can read a line with a rest.`],mistakes:[`Rushing past the rest. Put your hand on the icon for the full beat.`],challenge:`A pattern with two rests that still feels like music.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Write four beats. Include one rest.`,items:[{id:`line`,kind:`text`,label:`My pattern (ta, ti-ti, sh):`,lines:2},{id:`rest`,kind:`choice`,label:`A rest is`,choices:[`a beat of no sound`,`a chance to talk`,`the end of class`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:17,unit:`melody`,short:`Patterns`,title:`Four-Beat Patterns`,focus:`Put ta, ti-ti, and rest together. Read, clap, play.`,outcome:`Students read and perform four-beat patterns using ta, ti-ti, and rest.`,materials:[`Flash patterns`,`Rhythm sticks or laps`,`Studio on this site`],warmup:{title:`Flash cards`,detail:`Three cards: all tas; ta ti-ti ta ta; ta ta sh ta. See, wait, clap. No talking between.`},teach:[{title:`Eyes, then hands`,detail:`We read first in our heads. Then we clap. This stops blurting.`},{title:`Play it on sticks`,detail:`Same pattern, new timbre. Quiet sticks.`},{title:`Error hunt`,detail:`Teacher claps a written pattern with one mistake. Students catch it. Kind voices: “Beat 3 was a rest on the paper.”`},{title:`Link to a song`,detail:`Use a pattern as an ostinato under Rain, Rain — teacher sings, a small group plays the pattern twice.`}],activity:{title:`Write, trade, play`,detail:`Each child writes four beats using at least two kinds (ta / ti-ti / sh). Trade with a partner. Play your partner’s.`},close:`Perform two student patterns as a class. Stamp if reading is becoming independent.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Read with your eyes first`,detail:`No sound yet.`},{title:`Clap the card`,detail:`Then sticks if you have them.`},{title:`Catch a mistake`,detail:`Listen. Look at the paper.`},{title:`Write your own four beats`,detail:`Use two kinds at least.`},{title:`Play a friend’s pattern`,detail:`That is reading.`}],teacher:[`Walk the room and rewrite backwards letters or eight tas in one box.`,`Ostinato groups should be three or four children, not the whole loud class.`],checklist:[`I read before I clapped.`,`I wrote a four-beat pattern.`,`I played a friend’s pattern.`],mistakes:[`Patterns longer than four. Four is the job.`],challenge:`Play your pattern twice without a hole in the middle. That is an ostinato.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Write two different four-beat patterns.`,items:[{id:`p1`,kind:`text`,label:`Pattern A:`,lines:1},{id:`p2`,kind:`text`,label:`Pattern B:`,lines:1},{id:`kinds`,kind:`choice`,label:`I used`,choices:[`only ta`,`ta and ti-ti or rest`,`scribbles`]}]},studio:`rhythm`},{n:18,unit:`melody`,short:`Compose`,title:`My Four-Beat Pattern`,focus:`Hand-in project 3: one four-beat pattern the class can echo.`,outcome:`Students create a simple rhythmic composition using known elements.`,materials:[`Worksheet`,`Laps or sticks`,`A way to notate (icons)`],warmup:{title:`Echo warmup`,detail:`Teacher claps four, class echoes. Student volunteer claps four, class echoes. Set the project mechanic.`},teach:[{title:`The recipe`,detail:`Four beats. At least two of: ta, ti-ti, rest. Must be echoable — not so wild the class cannot copy.`},{title:`Practice alone`,detail:`Write. Clap. Fix. If you cannot clap it, change it.`},{title:`How we share`,detail:`Child claps. Class echoes once. Kind applause (two claps). Next child. You will not get through 24 solos in 12 minutes — do a row today, another row if you have extra time, or split with week 19’s warmup.`},{title:`If they freeze`,detail:`They may clap all tas. That still counts. Pride matters more than complexity.`}],activity:{title:`Project 3 — share`,detail:`As many students as time allows. Teacher notes who still needs a turn next class. Collect papers.`},close:`Name the musical bravery, not the fanciest pattern. Stamp project 3 as you go, or stamp the week when papers are in.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Write four beats`,detail:`Ta, ti-ti, sh. At least two kinds if you can.`},{title:`Clap it yourself`,detail:`If it is too hard, change it.`},{title:`Clap for the class`,detail:`Clear and once.`},{title:`Echo your friends`,detail:`Copy them carefully.`},{title:`Hand in the paper`,detail:`Name on it.`}],teacher:[`Do not force a solo from a child who is not ready. They can clap with you in unison as their share.`,`Rubric: four beats, readable, echoable. That is a 4-point load if you split as in the project sheet.`],checklist:[`My pattern is four beats.`,`I clapped it.`,`I echoed someone else.`],mistakes:[`Using this day to teach a new note. No new content.`],challenge:`Add your pattern under a known song as a quiet ostinato with two friends.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Project 3 — my four-beat pattern.`,items:[{id:`pattern`,kind:`text`,label:`Write ta / ti-ti / sh:`,lines:2},{id:`echo`,kind:`choice`,label:`The class could echo it`,choices:[`yes`,`I still need to make it simpler`,`not yet`]}]},studio:`rhythm`}],r=[{n:19,unit:`timbre`,short:`High low`,title:`High and Low`,focus:`Pitch can be high or low. We hear it, we show it with our bodies, and we sing it.`,outcome:`Students show that sounds may be high or low and match simple pitch changes with voice and movement.`,materials:[`Piano on this site or a real xylophone`,`Space to crouch and stretch`,`Board: high / low`],warmup:{title:`Mountain and valley`,detail:`Teacher slides a siren up. Students stretch tall. Siren down. Students crouch. No words. Do it four times.`},teach:[{title:`High is not loud`,detail:`A mouse can be high and quiet. A drum can be low and soft. Separate pitch from volume right away.`},{title:`Two notes only`,detail:`Play a high G and a low C (or so and do). Students point up or down. Then they sing “high” and “low” on those pitches.`},{title:`Xylophone or site piano`,detail:`If you have a glockenspiel, let two children play high and low while the class shows with bodies. If not, use the studio on this website.`},{title:`Song: Star Light`,detail:`Star Light, Star Bright sits in a small range. Listen for the drop. Trace it in the air.`}],activity:{title:`Sort the sound`,detail:`Teacher plays or sings high/low in mixed order. Students stand for high, sit for low. Then pairs take the site piano or a bell and quiz their partner.`},close:`Ask: “If I sing softly, can it still be high?” Yes. Stamp if bodies match pitch, not volume.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Stretch for high, crouch for low`,detail:`Follow the siren.`},{title:`Point up or down`,detail:`Listen to two notes.`},{title:`Sing high and low`,detail:`Soft is okay.`},{title:`Trace Star Light`,detail:`Finger draws the tune in the air.`},{title:`Quiz a partner`,detail:`Play one note. They show high or low.`}],teacher:[`Avoid “higher is better.” Low voices are music too.`,`A child who only mouths can still show with their body and be fully in.`],checklist:[`I can show high and low with my body.`,`I know high is not the same as loud.`,`I traced a melody in the air.`],mistakes:[`Using a tiny pitch difference they cannot hear. Make high and low obvious today.`],challenge:`Sing a so–mi “cuckoo” (high–low) and let the class echo.`,worksheet:{prompt:`High or low?`,items:[{id:`notloud`,kind:`choice`,label:`High means`,choices:[`up in pitch`,`loud`,`fast`]},{id:`show`,kind:`text`,label:`I show high by`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:20,unit:`timbre`,short:`Up and down`,title:`Melody Goes Up and Down`,focus:`A melody is a line. It can go up, down, or stay. We draw the line as we sing.`,outcome:`Students show melodic contour with movement and simple maps.`,materials:[`Paper for contour maps`,`A known so–mi song`,`Yarn optional`],warmup:{title:`Finger paint the air`,detail:`Teacher sings a slow so–mi–so. Students draw it in the air with one finger. Then a line that only goes up. Then only down.`},teach:[{title:`Contour means the shape`,detail:`We do not need note names to see the hill. Draw three maps on the board: hill, slide down, flat road.`},{title:`Map a known song`,detail:`See-Saw is a little up-down. Draw it together. Rain, Rain too.`},{title:`Same or different shape`,detail:`Sing two patterns. Thumbs up if the maps would look the same.`},{title:`Stay means repeats`,detail:`Ta ta on the same pitch is a flat line. That is still a melody.`}],activity:{title:`Draw the tune`,detail:`Students listen three times to a short pattern and draw one contour line. Share with a neighbour: did your hills match?`},close:`Hold up two student maps. Class sings them as best they can. Stamp if drawings show direction.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Draw the air tune`,detail:`One finger. Slow.`},{title:`Name hill, slide, or road`,detail:`Look at the board.`},{title:`Map See-Saw`,detail:`Copy the class map, then try your own.`},{title:`Listen for same or different`,detail:`Thumbs up if the shape matches.`},{title:`Draw one mystery tune`,detail:`Listen three times.`}],teacher:[`Accept wobbly lines. Direction is the outcome, not art.`,`Do not require staff paper.`],checklist:[`I can draw a melody going up or down.`,`I mapped a song we know.`,`I compared my map with a friend.`],mistakes:[`Asking them to write note names on the staff today.`],challenge:`Sing a friend’s map without being told the words.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Draw the shape of a tune you heard.`,items:[{id:`map`,kind:`text`,label:`My melody map (describe: hill / slide / road):`,lines:2},{id:`dir`,kind:`choice`,label:`See-Saw mostly`,choices:[`goes up and down`,`stays on one shout`,`has no shape`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:21,unit:`timbre`,short:`So–mi songs`,title:`So–Mi Songs`,focus:`Put high and low into real songs. So–mi is the Grade 1 singing home.`,outcome:`Students sing so–mi songs in tune with hand signs.`,materials:[`Rain Rain, See-Saw, Star Light`,`Hand-sign space`],warmup:{title:`Sign and sing`,detail:`Silent signs, class sings. Then sung pattern, class signs. Keep it in so–mi only.`},teach:[{title:`Three songs, one pair of pitches`,detail:`Rain Rain, See-Saw, Star Light. They are family. Hum one and ask which song it might be.`},{title:`Fix the sticky phrase`,detail:`Do not run whole songs if phrase two is out of tune. Echo that phrase three times, then go back.`},{title:`Alone, then group`,detail:`A volunteer may sing a so–mi hello. The group answers. Sitting solos are allowed.`},{title:`Add a beat`,detail:`Laps on the beat while we sing. Two jobs again — easier now than week 11.`}],activity:{title:`Song medley`,detail:`Sing all three songs with signs. Pick one to perform standing as if it were concert posture.`},close:`Which song felt easiest? That is their home song for later weeks. Stamp if signs and sound agree.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Warm up so and mi`,detail:`Hands and voice.`},{title:`Sing Rain, Rain`,detail:`Light voice.`},{title:`Sing See-Saw`,detail:`Small knee bend.`},{title:`Sing Star Light`,detail:`Quiet ending.`},{title:`Stand one of them`,detail:`Concert feet. Still hands unless there are actions.`}],teacher:[`If a class is shouty, sit and use a whisper-sing lead. They will drop.`,`Take a note of which song is strongest — that is a concert candidate for unit 6.`],checklist:[`I sang at least two so–mi songs.`,`My hand signs matched so and mi.`,`I used a singing voice, not a shout.`],mistakes:[`Adding la in the middle of a shaky so–mi. Wait one more week if they are not ready.`],challenge:`Lead See-Saw with only your hands. Class sings.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our so–mi songs.`,items:[{id:`songs`,kind:`text`,label:`Songs we sang today:`,lines:2},{id:`home`,kind:`choice`,label:`So is`,choices:[`higher`,`lower`,`louder`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:22,unit:`timbre`,short:`Add la`,title:`Add La`,focus:`La sits above so. Three pitches: so, mi, and la. Bounce High uses them.`,outcome:`Students respond to hand signals for so–mi–la and sing a three-pitch song.`,materials:[`Bounce High, Bounce Low`,`La hand sign (thumb up at top of head / curled so-hand higher — use one clear so-la-mi set and stick to it)`],warmup:{title:`So–mi neighbour`,detail:`Review so–mi. Then teacher sings so–la–so–mi. Students echo on “loo.”`},teach:[{title:`La is a step above so`,detail:`Show the sign higher than so. Students copy. Sing la lightly — it should not punch.`},{title:`Bounce High`,detail:`“Bounce high, bounce low, bounce the ball to Shiloh.” Teach by echo. Put a class name in. Keep the ball bounce on the beat if you use one.`},{title:`Sign the new note only`,detail:`First pass, only sign la when it happens. Then sign the whole song.`},{title:`Do not pile on do yet`,detail:`Three pitches is plenty. Hot Cross Buns can wait until they own la.`}],activity:{title:`Name toss`,detail:`Bounce High with a soft ball or beanbag. The named child sings the last “Shiloh” as their own name on the same tune if they want. Optional.`},close:`Silent signs: so–la–so–mi. Class sings. Stamp if la is in the neighbourhood of in-tune.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Show la`,detail:`Higher than so. Light voice.`},{title:`Echo so–la–so–mi`,detail:`On loo.`},{title:`Sing Bounce High`,detail:`Put in a friend’s name if the teacher does.`},{title:`Sign la when it happens`,detail:`Then try all the signs.`},{title:`Keep the beat in your bounce`,detail:`The ball is the beat, not a throw at someone.`}],teacher:[`Kodály la is a curved hand above so. If you use a simpler “higher so,” be consistent all year.`,`Beanbag underhand. No dodgeball.`],checklist:[`I can show la.`,`I sang Bounce High.`,`I know la is higher than so.`],mistakes:[`A high la that becomes a squeal. Model a gentle one.`],challenge:`Improvise four beats on so–mi–la with your voice. Class copies.`,worksheet:{prompt:`So, mi, and la.`,items:[{id:`order`,kind:`choice`,label:`The highest of so, mi, and la is`,choices:[`la`,`mi`,`so`]},{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`A song with la:`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:23,unit:`timbre`,short:`Hand signs`,title:`Hand Signs We Can Read`,focus:`The teacher’s hands are a score. We read so–mi–la without words.`,outcome:`Students read simple so–mi–la patterns from hand signs and sing them back.`,materials:[`Clear sightlines`,`No other props`],warmup:{title:`Copy my hands, no voice`,detail:`Silent sign strings of four. Students copy. Then add voice.`},teach:[{title:`Hands are the music`,detail:`You are not guessing. You are reading. Eyes on hands, then sound.`},{title:`Four-beat reading`,detail:`so so mi so. so la so mi. Rest can be closed fists. Keep it slow.`},{title:`Student conductor`,detail:`A child signs a pattern they know. Class sings. Help them stand where everyone can see.`},{title:`From signs to a song`,detail:`Sign the first phrase of Rain, Rain. They should recognize it. Magic moment — name it.`}],activity:{title:`Sign theatre`,detail:`Three student conductors. Class sings each pattern twice. Teacher sneaks in a known song phrase to be discovered.`},close:`All eyes closed. Teacher sings so–mi–la–so. Students sign what they heard. Stamp if signs match.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Watch first`,detail:`Hands, then voice.`},{title:`Sing four-beat patterns`,detail:`so, mi, la only.`},{title:`Conduct if you want`,detail:`Big clear signs.`},{title:`Guess the song from signs`,detail:`Rain, Rain or See-Saw maybe.`},{title:`Sign what you hear`,detail:`Eyes can close for the last one.`}],teacher:[`Stand on a chair only if it is safe and allowed. Otherwise a stool or just the front.`,`Fist rest is optional. Empty air also works.`],checklist:[`I watched the hands before I sang.`,`I sang a so–mi–la pattern from signs.`,`I recognized a song from its signs.`],mistakes:[`Patterns longer than four. Reading falls apart.`],challenge:`Sign a whole phrase of Bounce High. Can the class name it?`,worksheet:{prompt:`Reading hands.`,items:[{id:`read`,kind:`choice`,label:`When the teacher signs, I`,choices:[`watch, then sing`,`look at the floor`,`invent any tune`]},{id:`pattern`,kind:`text`,label:`A pattern we sang (write so mi la):`,lines:1}]},studio:`piano`},{n:24,unit:`timbre`,short:`Little tune`,title:`A Little Melody`,focus:`Make a tiny so–mi–la tune, four beats long, and sing it for a friend.`,outcome:`Students create a short melody using known pitches and perform it.`,materials:[`Worksheet`,`Site piano`,`Optional bells`],warmup:{title:`Pitch bank`,detail:`Class sings so, mi, la as you point. Then they tap those three on the studio piano.`},teach:[{title:`Recipe`,detail:`Four beats. Use so, mi, and maybe la. Start and end on so or mi so it feels finished.`},{title:`Voice first`,detail:`If they write letters they cannot sing, it is not done. Hum it. Then write so/mi/la under four beats.`},{title:`Add rhythm they know`,detail:`Each beat is ta (one pitch) or ti-ti (two pitches). Rest is allowed.`},{title:`Share with a partner, not always the whole class`,detail:`Grade 1 composition is quiet and small. A few volunteers can share out.`}],activity:{title:`Write and hum`,detail:`Compose. Try on piano. Teach your partner. Two volunteers share.`},close:`Collect tunes. We may reuse one in the concert unit. Stamp when papers have four beats of pitches.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Choose four beats of so, mi, la`,detail:`Start on so or mi if you can.`},{title:`Hum it`,detail:`If you cannot hum it, change it.`},{title:`Try it on the piano`,detail:`G is so, E is mi, A is la on our studio.`},{title:`Teach a partner`,detail:`They echo you.`},{title:`Write it down`,detail:`Name on the paper.`}],teacher:[`Do not require staff notation. Letter names or so-mi-la words are enough.`,`Save two strong little tunes for week 33’s class piece.`],checklist:[`My tune is four beats.`,`I can hum it.`,`A friend echoed it.`],mistakes:[`Eight different pitches. Three is the bank.`],challenge:`Put your tune over a ta ta ta ta beat that a friend pats.`,worksheet:{prompt:`My little melody.`,items:[{id:`tune`,kind:`text`,label:`Four beats (write so / mi / la):`,lines:2},{id:`start`,kind:`choice`,label:`I started on`,choices:[`so or mi`,`a shout`,`I am not sure`]}]},studio:`piano`},{n:25,unit:`form`,short:`Body perc`,title:`Body Percussion`,focus:`Clap, pat, stamp, snap — four sounds we already own. Transfer the beat and simple patterns onto the body.`,outcome:`Students accompany singing with body percussion (beat and simple patterns) with care.`,materials:[`Open space`,`A known song`,`Quiet floor for stamps`],warmup:{title:`Copy four`,detail:`Clap clap pat pat. Class copies. Snap if they can; many Grade 1s cannot snap — two-finger tap on the palm is a snap substitute.`},teach:[{title:`Name the four`,detail:`Clap, pat (laps), stamp (gentle), snap or tap. Volume rule: we should still hear the song.`},{title:`Beat on the body`,detail:`Sing Hello or Rain, Rain. Beat in pats. Then beat in quiet stamps.`},{title:`Pattern on the body`,detail:`ti-ti ta rest on clap-clap pat open. This links unit 3 to instruments next week.`},{title:`Safety`,detail:`Stamps are not jumps. Snaps are not at someone’s ear.`}],activity:{title:`Body ostinato`,detail:`A small group repeats clap-pat-clap-rest while the class sings. Swap groups.`},close:`Freeze in a body-percussion statue. Stamp if volume stayed kind.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Copy clap, pat, stamp, tap`,detail:`Gentle.`},{title:`Put the beat in your laps`,detail:`Sing at the same time.`},{title:`Try a pattern`,detail:`ti-ti ta sh.`},{title:`Ostinato group`,detail:`Repeat without speeding up.`},{title:`Hear the singers`,detail:`If you cannot hear them, you are too loud.`}],teacher:[`Substitute snap from day one so no one feels broken for not snapping.`,`Carpet classes: stamp becomes a toe tap.`],checklist:[`I can clap, pat, and tap the beat.`,`I used a quiet stamp or toe tap.`,`I could still hear the song.`],mistakes:[`A complicated cup game. Too soon.`],challenge:`Invent a four-beat body pattern and teach your row.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Body percussion.`,items:[{id:`sounds`,kind:`text`,label:`Four body sounds we used:`,lines:2},{id:`vol`,kind:`choice`,label:`While we play we should still`,choices:[`hear the singers`,`cover the singers`,`stop the song`]}]}},{n:26,unit:`form`,short:`Instruments`,title:`Classroom Instruments with Care`,focus:`Rhythm sticks, shakers, bells, drums. How we hold them, how we rest them, how we play a beat.`,outcome:`Students play a steady beat using rhythm instruments and explore their sounds.`,materials:[`Whatever you have: sticks, shakers, triangles, hand drums`,`Rest position taught on day one of instruments`],warmup:{title:`Rest position`,detail:`Instruments on the floor or on shoulders, not making sound. Practice pick up, play four beats, rest. That loop is the whole classroom management system.`},teach:[{title:`One job today: the beat`,detail:`Not the words. Even sounds with the drum or with me.`},{title:`How each one speaks`,detail:`Sticks tap. Shakers shake once per beat (not a sandstorm). Bells ding and wait. Drums are open palm, not fists, unless it is a proper hand drum technique you teach.`},{title:`Quiet is a skill`,detail:`The best players can play so the song is still the star.`},{title:`Passing out and in`,detail:`You pass out. They do not help themselves. Collect the same way. Build this ritual now.`}],activity:{title:`Rotate colours`,detail:`If you have four types, four stations, two minutes each, beat only, then rest. A song underneath the whole time.`},close:`Collect. Count. Thank the instruments. Stamp if rest position worked.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Rest position`,detail:`No sound until the start hand.`},{title:`Play the beat`,detail:`Even. With the teacher.`},{title:`Treat each instrument the way it wants`,detail:`Shake, tap, or ding.`},{title:`Hear the song`,detail:`Play under it, not over it.`},{title:`Give it back with two hands`,detail:`Wait to be asked.`}],teacher:[`Fewer instruments is better than a riot. Half the class can body-perc and switch.`,`Triangles need a fat yarn handle and a rule: one ding per beat.`],checklist:[`I used rest position.`,`I played an even beat.`,`I gave the instrument back with care.`],mistakes:[`Passing out before teaching rest. Always rest first.`],challenge:`Play the beat with eyes on the conductor, not on the instrument.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Instruments.`,items:[{id:`used`,kind:`text`,label:`An instrument I played:`,lines:1},{id:`rest`,kind:`choice`,label:`Rest position means`,choices:[`no sound until the start`,`practice as loud as I want`,`hide the instrument`]}]},studio:`quiz`},{n:27,unit:`form`,short:`Name sound`,title:`Name, Sound, Picture`,focus:`Match an instrument’s name, its sound, and what it looks like. Alberta Grade 1 loves this game.`,outcome:`Students explore the sound of various instruments and match names, sounds, and pictures.`,materials:[`3–6 real instruments or pictures`,`Studio quiz on this site`,`A cloth to hide one instrument`],warmup:{title:`Mystery bag`,detail:`Hide one instrument. Play it unseen. Students guess the name. Reveal.`},teach:[{title:`Three cards for each`,detail:`Word, picture, sound. We connect all three. Start with drum, shaker, bell, sticks.`},{title:`How it makes the sound`,detail:`Hit, shake, scrape. Let them name the action.`},{title:`High or low colour`,detail:`Bell vs drum. Link to week 19 without a lecture.`},{title:`Use the studio`,detail:`The website can play drum, shaker, bell, stick if you have no kit today.`}],activity:{title:`Match the table`,detail:`Stations: hear, then walk to the picture card. Or a paper match on the worksheet. Then the site quiz in pairs if devices exist — otherwise teacher-led on the projector.`},close:`Four sounds, four names, fast. Stamp if most matches are right.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Guess the mystery sound`,detail:`Listen before you shout the name.`},{title:`Say how it is played`,detail:`Hit, shake, or scrape.`},{title:`Match picture to sound`,detail:`Walk or point.`},{title:`Try the studio buttons`,detail:`If we use the website.`},{title:`Use the real name`,detail:`Drum, shaker, bell, sticks.`}],teacher:[`Include at least one instrument they have not played yet so listening stays sharp.`,`If you have a guest from a local ensemble, this is a good week — but the lesson stands without one.`],checklist:[`I matched a sound to a name.`,`I matched a picture to an instrument.`,`I can tell hit, shake, or scrape.`],mistakes:[`Ten instruments. Four is a Grade 1 set.`],challenge:`Be the mystery player. Class guesses with eyes closed.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Name, sound, picture.`,items:[{id:`hit`,kind:`choice`,label:`A drum is usually`,choices:[`hit`,`blown like a flute`,`plugged in`]},{id:`names`,kind:`text`,label:`Instruments we named today:`,lines:2}]},studio:`quiz`},{n:28,unit:`form`,short:`Found sounds`,title:`Found Sounds and New Ways`,focus:`Music can come from pencils, paper, chairs (gently), and invented instruments. We explore with rules.`,outcome:`Students explore environmental and found sounds and play them with care.`,materials:[`Paper, pencils, a metal water bottle, a box`,`A “no” list: bodies, other people’s things, anything that can break`],warmup:{title:`Paper orchestra`,detail:`Each child has one paper. Explore: tap, wave, rip a tiny corner if allowed. Freeze. Which sound was your favourite?`},teach:[{title:`Found sound has rules`,detail:`We do not wreck school things. We do not use friends as drums. We try, we share, we put it back.`},{title:`Same object, new way`,detail:`A pencil can tap, scrape, or roll. That is “new ways” — Alberta language, kid-sized.`},{title:`Build a tiny piece`,detail:`Four beats of found sound as an ostinato under a known chant.`},{title:`Invented instrument`,detail:`Two objects together (bottle + pencil). Name it. Play a beat. Bow.`}],activity:{title:`Composer pairs`,detail:`Pairs pick two found sounds, write a four-beat idea (icons), perform once.`},close:`Reset the room better than we found it. That is still musicianship. Stamp if exploration stayed inside the rules.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Explore paper`,detail:`Find two different sounds.`},{title:`Hear the rules again`,detail:`Kind to things and people.`},{title:`Invent a pair sound`,detail:`Give it a name.`},{title:`Play four beats under a chant`,detail:`Quiet enough to hear words.`},{title:`Put everything back`,detail:`Musicians tidy.`}],teacher:[`If admin is nervous, skip rips and only tap.`,`This week makes a good photo for families if your school allows.`],checklist:[`I found two sounds from one object.`,`I followed the care rules.`,`I helped tidy.`],mistakes:[`Dumping a junk box with no rules. Rules first, then wonder.`],challenge:`Your invented instrument plays only on the rests of a class pattern.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Found sounds.`,items:[{id:`object`,kind:`text`,label:`I made a sound with:`,lines:1},{id:`way`,kind:`text`,label:`A new way I played it:`,lines:1},{id:`rule`,kind:`choice`,label:`Found sounds must be`,choices:[`safe for people and things`,`as loud as recess`,`taken home without asking`]}]}},{n:29,unit:`form`,short:`Fast slow`,title:`Fast and Slow, Loud and Quiet`,focus:`Tempo and dynamics. We change the music on purpose and still stay together.`,outcome:`Students show loud/quiet and fast/slow and follow a conductor for both.`,materials:[`Known song`,`Cue cards: fast / slow / loud / quiet`,`Drum`],warmup:{title:`Follow my feet`,detail:`Walk slow, walk faster, freeze. Then clap piano (quiet) and forte (loud) — use the kid words plus the music words once.`},teach:[{title:`Two pairs`,detail:`Fast/slow is speed. Loud/quiet is volume. Do not mix the cards at first.`},{title:`The song stays the same`,detail:`Rain, Rain slow. Rain, Rain faster. Same tune. Then quiet vs strong. Same tune.`},{title:`Conductor tools`,detail:`Small hand = quiet. Big hand = stronger. Slow scoop vs quick tick.`},{title:`Piano and forte`,detail:`Say the words once. They do not need to spell them. Quiet and loud remain on the cards.`}],activity:{title:`Four-card song`,detail:`A student helper shows a card. Class sings the known song that way. Rotate helpers.`},close:`One child conducts quiet to loud on a long loo. Stamp if the class actually changes.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Walk slow, then faster`,detail:`Still a beat.`},{title:`Clap quiet, then stronger`,detail:`Not a scream.`},{title:`Sing the song four ways`,detail:`Watch the card.`},{title:`Name piano and forte`,detail:`Quiet and loud.`},{title:`Conduct one change`,detail:`If you are the helper, show the card early.`}],teacher:[`Faster + louder often glue together. Pull them apart with separate rounds.`,`Never ask Grade 1 for fortissimo. Strong and kind is the ceiling.`],checklist:[`I can sing slow and faster.`,`I can sing quiet and stronger.`,`I watched the conductor.`],mistakes:[`Faster meaning sloppy. Keep the words clear.`],challenge:`Change in the middle of the song, not just at the start.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Expression.`,items:[{id:`tempo`,kind:`choice`,label:`Fast and slow are about`,choices:[`speed`,`how high the notes are`,`the instrument’s name`]},{id:`dyn`,kind:`choice`,label:`Piano means`,choices:[`quiet`,`the loudest possible`,`stop`]}]}},{n:30,unit:`form`,short:`Listen well`,title:`Listen with Respect`,focus:`We listen to music of First Nations, Métis, and Inuit peoples, and music from many places, with care. We do not copy what is not ours to copy.`,outcome:`Students listen with purpose to music of diverse cultures, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit music, and practise respectful audience skills.`,materials:[`One short recording you have rights to play (school library, licensed service, or a visiting artist)`,`A map of Turtle Island / Canada`,`Listening map paper`],warmup:{title:`How we sit to listen`,detail:`Still bodies, looking at the speaker or the player. One listen with eyes. Name what we will listen for: drum, voice, fiddle, or dance feet.`},teach:[{title:`Respect first`,detail:`Some songs are sacred or family-owned. In this class we listen. We do not pretend, costume, or make up “Indigenous-sounding” chants. If a local Nation or Métis artist has shared a song for schools, we credit them by name.`},{title:`One focus listen`,detail:`Play 45–90 seconds. Ask: drum or fiddle? Fast or slow? For dancing or for sitting? Accept kid words.`},{title:`Métis fiddle and jig is one good door`,detail:`If you use a jig, students may pat a beat or try a simple step only if you are teaching a school-appropriate dance, not a mockery. Movement is optional. Listening is the job.`},{title:`Many cultures`,detail:`If time, a second short piece from another place the class has a real link to (a classmate’s family, a book you read). Same respectful frame.`}],story:{title:`The listening chair`,body:`Sam wanted to bang along the first time the drum came through the speaker. Ms. Cole lifted the quiet hand. “This drum already has players,” she said. “Today we borrow our ears, not their song.” Sam sat on his hands and heard the pattern he had missed. After, he said the drum was like walking. That was enough.`},activity:{title:`Draw what you heard`,detail:`A listening map: dots for drum, line for voice or fiddle. Share one noticing with a partner. No performing of the recording.`},close:`Credit the artists again. “Thank you for the listen.” Stamp if the room stayed in listening mode.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Sit like an audience`,detail:`Still. Ready ears.`},{title:`Know the rule`,detail:`We listen. We do not copy sacred or family songs.`},{title:`Listen once for a job`,detail:`Drum? Voice? Fast or slow?`},{title:`Draw a listening map`,detail:`Simple.`},{title:`Say one noticing`,detail:`Kind words. Credit the musicians.`}],teacher:[`Do not use random uncredited clips. Name the performer and Nation or community if it is known and appropriate.`,`If you are not sure a song is okay to teach, do not teach it. Listening with credit is the safe, good Grade 1 move.`,`Invite a local artist through proper school channels if you can — even a 15-minute visit beats a shaky recording.`],checklist:[`I sat and listened.`,`I can tell one thing I heard.`,`I know we do not copy songs that are not ours to sing.`],mistakes:[`Making feather headbands or fake drums. Never.`,`Calling all First Nations music “powwow.” Be specific or stay general with respect.`],challenge:`Write a thank-you sentence to the musicians we heard.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Listening with respect.`,items:[{id:`heard`,kind:`text`,label:`I heard:`,lines:2},{id:`rule`,kind:`choice`,label:`In this lesson we`,choices:[`listen and credit the musicians`,`make up a pretend sacred song`,`talk over the recording`]}]},faith:`Optional: listening without grabbing is a way to honour our neighbours.`},{n:31,unit:`concert`,short:`Same different`,title:`Same and Different`,focus:`Form we can feel: this part is the same, that part is different. Echo and verse live here.`,outcome:`Students identify same and different phrases and simple call-and-response form.`,materials:[`Two coloured scarves or cards: SAME / DIFFERENT`,`A song with two phrases`],warmup:{title:`Copy or change`,detail:`Teacher claps a pattern. Second time is either identical or one beat different. Thumbs.`},teach:[{title:`Phrases are musical sentences`,detail:`Sing Rain, Rain phrase 1, phrase 2. Are they the same? Mostly yes with a small tail. Keep it honest.`},{title:`A song with a chorus`,detail:`If you have a simple verse/chorus (even a hello song with a repeated line), hold SAME on the repeat.`},{title:`Call and response is a form`,detail:`Teacher call, class response. That is not just a trick — it is a shape. Name it.`},{title:`Map it with colour`,detail:`Blue card each time the same phrase returns. Yellow for a new one.`}],activity:{title:`Card holders`,detail:`Two students hold SAME and DIFFERENT. Class sings. Cards pop up. Switch helpers.`},close:`Ask for a song that feels like echo (same) vs a song that tells two different ideas. Stamp if cards match the music.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Thumbs for copy or change`,detail:`Listen to claps.`},{title:`Sing a song in two phrases`,detail:`Are they the same?`},{title:`Hold a form card`,detail:`Up at the right time.`},{title:`Be the call or the response`,detail:`Wait for your job.`},{title:`Name the shape`,detail:`Same, different, or echo.`}],teacher:[`ABA can wait. Same/different is the Grade 1 form win.`,`Charlie Over the Ocean is a strong call-response if you need a new song.`],checklist:[`I can tell same from different.`,`I know echo is a kind of same.`,`I helped show form with a card.`],mistakes:[`Graphing five sections. Two is enough.`],challenge:`Make a call. The class must respond with something different on purpose.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Same or different?`,items:[{id:`echo`,kind:`choice`,label:`Echo is usually`,choices:[`the same as the leader`,`always a brand new song`,`silence`]},{id:`shape`,kind:`text`,label:`A song we mapped today:`,lines:1}]}},{n:32,unit:`concert`,short:`Call back`,title:`Call and Response`,focus:`A leader sings, the group answers. We practise being both.`,outcome:`Students perform call and response with accurate waiting, matching, and kind leadership.`,materials:[`Charlie Over the Ocean or a hello call`,`Circle`],warmup:{title:`Name call`,detail:`Teacher: “Charlie over the ocean.” Class: echo. Then a child’s name in the call if the song allows. Keep it short.`},teach:[{title:`Jobs`,detail:`Leader waits after the call. Group does not steal the call. If you are not the leader, you are the response — that is a real job.`},{title:`Teach the song`,detail:`Charlie Over the Ocean (or Kye Kye Kule). Echo by phrase, then call-response in two groups, then with a student leader.`},{title:`Matching pitch still matters`,detail:`The response should sound like the call’s cousin, not a yell.`},{title:`Kind leaders`,detail:`The leader faces the group, uses a singing voice, and thanks the group after.`}],activity:{title:`Many leaders`,detail:`As many student calls as time allows. Sitting leaders welcome.`},close:`Group response without a reminder. Stamp if waiting is clean.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Learn the call`,detail:`Echo the teacher.`},{title:`Be the group`,detail:`Wait. Then answer.`},{title:`Lead if you want`,detail:`Singing voice. Face your friends.`},{title:`Match, don’t shout`,detail:`The answer is music.`},{title:`Thank the group`,detail:`Leaders are polite.`}],teacher:[`Skip ocean if water language is a classroom issue; use a hello call instead.`,`Kye Kye Kule: credit it as a Ghanaian game song commonly shared in schools, teach respectfully, keep the game gentle.`],checklist:[`I waited for my job.`,`I used a singing voice in the response.`,`I can be a leader or a group member.`],mistakes:[`Letting 24 children run a chasing game. Sit to sing, then a small movement if any.`],challenge:`Invent a two-beat call on so–mi. Class answers so–mi–so.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Call and response.`,items:[{id:`job`,kind:`choice`,label:`If I am not the leader I`,choices:[`sing the response`,`talk`,`start a new game`]},{id:`song`,kind:`text`,label:`Our call-and-response song:`,lines:1}]}},{n:33,unit:`concert`,short:`Choose`,title:`Choose What We Will Share`,focus:`Pick two songs and one played piece for the sharing concert. Rehearse the order.`,outcome:`Students help select repertoire and practise a simple concert order.`,materials:[`List of known songs`,`The four-beat patterns / little melodies from earlier projects`,`Board: 1, 2, 3`],warmup:{title:`Memory lane`,detail:`Hum four known songs. Students raise hands for the ones they feel ready to share.`},teach:[{title:`What makes a good share song`,detail:`We know the words. We can start and stop. We still like it. Not the newest, hardest thing.`},{title:`Vote with kindness`,detail:`We may not pick everyone’s first choice. We pick what this class can do well together.`},{title:`The played piece`,detail:`Body percussion ostinato, a found-sound pattern, or a student four-beat echoed by all. One only.`},{title:`Write the order`,detail:`Song, played piece, song. Practise walking to standing spots between them. That transition is the concert.`}],activity:{title:`Draft run`,detail:`Stand, piece 1, sit or stay, piece 2, piece 3, bow (a small nod). Time it. Aim under five minutes.`},close:`Write the program on the board and leave it up. Stamp if the order survived one full run.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Name a song you feel ready for`,detail:`Honest.`},{title:`Accept the class choices`,detail:`Kind even if yours was not picked.`},{title:`Learn the order`,detail:`1, 2, 3.`},{title:`Practise the standing spots`,detail:`Quiet feet.`},{title:`Nod at the end`,detail:`That is our bow.`}],teacher:[`You may override a vote if the favourite song is still a shout. You are the musician in the room.`,`Invite a buddy class or families for week 35 now.`],checklist:[`I know our three pieces.`,`I know the order.`,`I practised a small bow.`],mistakes:[`Adding a fourth piece. Three is plenty.`],challenge:`Be the announcer: say the name of piece 1 in a talking voice.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Our program.`,items:[{id:`p1`,kind:`text`,label:`Piece 1:`,lines:1},{id:`p2`,kind:`text`,label:`Piece 2:`,lines:1},{id:`p3`,kind:`text`,label:`Piece 3:`,lines:1}]}},{n:34,unit:`concert`,short:`Audience`,title:`How to Be an Audience`,focus:`When we are not the ones singing, we still have a job: still, kind, clap at the end.`,outcome:`Students demonstrate appropriate audience behaviour and follow a conductor as performers.`,materials:[`A short audio or a teacher solo`,`Practice chairs as “audience”`],warmup:{title:`Bad audience / good audience`,detail:`Teacher models wiggling and talking. Students freeze it. Then model still and watching. Name the difference. Keep it funny, not shaming.`},teach:[{title:`Audience rules`,detail:`Voices off. Bodies on the chair. Eyes on the players. Clap when the piece is done, not in the rest in the middle. No phones — say it if families will attend.`},{title:`Performer rules`,detail:`Wait for the start. If you mess up, keep going. Smile at the end.`},{title:`Rehearse both jobs`,detail:`Half class performs piece 1, half is audience. Swap.`},{title:`The clap`,detail:`Two or three seconds of clapping, then rest. Not a stomp-clap chant unless you choose that as a class.`}],activity:{title:`Dress rehearsal without costumes`,detail:`Full order. Real start and stop. Teacher only conducts. No extra teaching talk if you can help it.`},close:`One compliment from audience to performers, one from performers to audience. Stamp if both jobs appeared.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Practise still sitting`,detail:`Audience job.`},{title:`Clap at the end only`,detail:`Wait for the last sound.`},{title:`Perform your piece`,detail:`Keep going if you wobble.`},{title:`Swap jobs`,detail:`Both are music.`},{title:`Full order once`,detail:`Like the real share.`}],teacher:[`If families come next week, send the family letter today.`,`Plan where coats and siblings go. Grade 1 concerts fail in the hallway, not on the songs.`],checklist:[`I can be a still audience.`,`I clap at the end.`,`I kept going as a performer.`],mistakes:[`A lecture on etiquette with no practice. Do the jobs.`],challenge:`Be the person who starts the clap when the conductor’s hands drop.`,worksheet:{prompt:`Audience and performer.`,items:[{id:`aud`,kind:`choice`,label:`An audience`,choices:[`watches and claps at the end`,`sings the whole time from their seat`,`walks around`]},{id:`perf`,kind:`choice`,label:`If I make a mistake I`,choices:[`keep going`,`stop the concert`,`laugh at my friend`]}]}},{n:35,unit:`concert`,short:`Share`,title:`Sharing Concert`,focus:`Hand-in project 4. We share two songs and one played piece with our audience.`,outcome:`Students sing in unison with growing expression and perform for an audience with confidence.`,materials:[`Program on the board`,`Spots marked`,`Audience (buddy class, families, or stuffed animals if needed)`],warmup:{title:`Body and breath`,detail:`Shake out. Tall. One so–mi on loo. Quiet hand. That is the whole warmup — do not tire them.`},teach:[{title:`This is the share, not a new lesson`,detail:`No new songs. Remind order. Remind still feet. Remind smile.`},{title:`If the audience is late`,detail:`Sing the warmup song sitting. Do not lose the room.`},{title:`You run it`,detail:`Announcer (teacher or a child) names piece 1. Conduct. Nod. Piece 2. Piece 3. Thank the audience.`},{title:`After`,detail:`Sit. Two comments from you that are specific (“Your rest in the rhythm piece was real silence”).`}],activity:{title:`Project 4 — share`,detail:`Do the program. If no visitors, invite another class or perform for the principal on a walk-by. Stuffed animal audience is a real backup and still counts.`},close:`Thank the musicians. Stamp project 4. Take a still photo if allowed.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Stand on your spot`,detail:`Quiet feet.`},{title:`Watch the start`,detail:`Breathe with the teacher.`},{title:`Sing or play our three pieces`,detail:`Keep going.`},{title:`Nod at the end`,detail:`Then sit for the thank-you.`},{title:`Be a kind audience if others play`,detail:`Still bodies.`}],teacher:[`Have water and a tissue. Have a second adult if families come.`,`Rubric: unison, a bit of expression, audience manners. Do not dock a child for a wobble.`],checklist:[`I stood on my spot.`,`I watched the teacher.`,`I shared our music.`],mistakes:[`Stopping to reteach mid-concert. Keep going.`],challenge:`Help a friend find their spot without talking.`,worksheet:{prompt:`After our share.`,items:[{id:`felt`,kind:`text`,label:`I felt:`,lines:2},{id:`proud`,kind:`text`,label:`I am proud that we:`,lines:2}]}},{n:36,unit:`concert`,short:`What I know`,title:`What I Know About Music Now`,focus:`Remember the year: beat, ta and ti-ti, so–mi–la, instruments, listening with respect. Celebrate.`,outcome:`Students reflect on singing, playing, moving, and listening growth from the year.`,materials:[`A few favourite songs`,`Worksheets from the year if you kept them`,`A simple celebration (stickers are optional, singing is the treat)`],warmup:{title:`Medley of us`,detail:`Thirty seconds each of four favourite songs. No teaching. Just the joy.`},teach:[{title:`Name the big ideas`,detail:`Beat. Long and short. High and low. Kind listening. Unison. Let students finish your sentences.`},{title:`Show what you know`,detail:`Stations if you can: clap a four-beat card, sign so–mi, play a beat on a shaker, sit as audience for a 20-second listen.`},{title:`A letter to next year’s class`,detail:`One sentence: “In Grade 1 music you will…” Teacher can scribe for those who need it.`},{title:`Thank you`,detail:`Thank their voices. Thank their waiting. Thank their families if that is your culture.`}],activity:{title:`Portfolio page`,detail:`Worksheet plus one last song standing. Optional: they teach a so–mi hello to a stuffed guest or a Grade 1 buddy from another class.`},close:`Sing the very first hello song from week 1. Notice how it feels now. Stamp the year.`,studentSteps:[{title:`Sing a favourite`,detail:`With the class.`},{title:`Show a skill at a station`,detail:`Beat, signs, or listening.`},{title:`Tell what you know`,detail:`One sentence is enough.`},{title:`Write or say a tip for next year`,detail:`Kind and true.`},{title:`Sing hello like week 1`,detail:`Notice your singing voice.`}],teacher:[`This is not a test week. It is a mirror.`,`Send leftover recordings home only with permission.`,`Note two students who grew quietly — tell them out loud.`],checklist:[`I sang a favourite song.`,`I showed one music skill.`,`I said or wrote something I know about music.`],mistakes:[`A paper exam. Grade 1 shows music by doing it.`],challenge:`Teach a grown-up at home the quiet hand and a so–mi hello.`,worksheet:{prompt:`What I know about music now.`,items:[{id:`know`,kind:`text`,label:`I know that music can:`,lines:2},{id:`love`,kind:`text`,label:`A song I love:`,lines:1},{id:`tip`,kind:`text`,label:`A tip for next year’s Grade 1:`,lines:2}]},faith:`Optional: we give thanks for voices, for waiting, and for music we were allowed to share.`}],i=[{id:`voice`,n:1,title:`Voice & Echo`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 1 – 6`,start:1,end:6,blurb:`Speaking voice, singing voice, echo games, and so–mi.`,outcome:`Students distinguish speaking and singing voices and echo simple songs in tune.`},{id:`rhythm`,n:2,title:`Steady Beat`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 7 – 12`,start:7,end:12,blurb:`Feel, walk, and play a steady beat. Strong beats and freeze games.`,outcome:`Students show that music may move to a steady beat, with the body and with instruments.`},{id:`melody`,n:3,title:`Long, Short, Silence`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 13 – 18`,start:13,end:18,blurb:`Ta, ti-ti, and rest. Four-beat patterns a Grade 1 can clap and write with icons.`,outcome:`Students hear long sounds, short sounds, and silences, and clap ta, ti-ti, and rest.`},{id:`timbre`,n:4,title:`High & Low`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 19 – 24`,start:19,end:24,blurb:`Melody goes up and down. So–mi–la hand signs and tiny tunes.`,outcome:`Students show high and low, and sing so–mi and so–mi–la with hand signs.`},{id:`form`,n:5,title:`Instruments & Colour`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 25 – 30`,start:25,end:30,blurb:`Body percussion, classroom instruments, found sounds, loud and quiet, fast and slow.`,outcome:`Students explore instrument sounds, match name–sound–picture, and play a beat with care.`},{id:`concert`,n:6,title:`Songs to Share`,weeksLabel:`Weeks 31 – 36`,start:31,end:36,blurb:`Same and different, call and response, audience manners, and a class sharing.`,outcome:`Students sing in unison with expression and listen as a kind audience.`}],a=[{n:1,title:`Our class song`,due:`Week 6`,week:6,blurb:`The class sings one echo or so–mi song in unison, standing tall, with a start and a stop together.`,must:[`Most voices use a singing voice, not a shout`,`The class starts and stops with the teacher’s hand`,`Students can tell the song’s words to a partner`],rubric:[`Singing voice 2`,`Starting and stopping together 1`,`Knowing the words 1`]},{n:2,title:`Beat parade`,due:`Week 12`,week:12,blurb:`Students walk, pat, or play a steady beat while a known song is sung. Freeze on the silence.`,must:[`Beat stays even (not the rhythm of the words)`,`Freeze on the rest or the teacher’s stop`,`Safe feet and quiet instruments`],rubric:[`Steady beat 2`,`Freeze / control 1`,`Safe, careful playing 1`]},{n:3,title:`Four-beat pattern`,due:`Week 18`,week:18,blurb:`Each student claps or plays one four-beat pattern using ta, ti-ti, and rest, then the class echoes it.`,must:[`Pattern is four beats long`,`Uses at least two of: ta, ti-ti, rest`,`Class can echo it once`],rubric:[`Correct length 1`,`Clear ta / ti-ti / rest 2`,`Echoable for the class 1`]},{n:4,title:`Sharing concert`,due:`Week 35`,week:35,blurb:`The class shares two songs and one rhythm or instrument piece with a small audience (buddy class, families, or just the principal).`,must:[`Two songs sung in unison`,`One played or clapped piece`,`Audience job practiced the week before`],rubric:[`Unison singing 2`,`Expression (loud/quiet or still/moving) 1`,`Audience manners 1`]}],o=[...n,...r];function s(e){return o.find(t=>t.n===e)??o[0]}function c(e){return i.find(t=>t.id===e)??i[0]}function l(e){return o.filter(t=>t.unit===e)}function u(e){return c(s(e).unit)}function d(t){return e[t]??[]}function f(e){return t[e]}var p=[{element:`Rhythm`,items:[`Demonstrate pulse (individual / group).`,`Identify pulse as ta.`,`Differentiate sound and silence; identify silence as rest.`,`Experience and identify fast / slow.`,`Identify ti-ti / ta.`,`Experience meter (2/4, 4/4, 6/8).`]},{element:`Melody`,items:[`Differentiate high / low and experience up / down.`,`Build a folk, seasonal, multicultural, and patriotic repertoire.`,`Experience so–mi and so–mi–la melodies.`,`Identify SM / SML tones and hand signs.`,`Demonstrate contour.`]},{element:`Harmony`,items:[`Develop proper mallet technique.`,`Demonstrate a simple bordun (chord).`,`Experience ostinato.`]},{element:`Form`,items:[`Experience same / different.`,`Identify phrase, introduction, and the repeat sign.`,`Identify same / different as AB (and ABA).`]},{element:`Timbre`,items:[`Identify unpitched percussion.`,`Differentiate vocal qualities: speak, sing, whisper, shout.`,`Demonstrate loud / quiet.`,`Classify unpitched as wood / metal / skin.`,`Identify four levels of body percussion: snap, clap, pat, stamp.`,`Classify pitched percussion as wood / metal.`]}],m=[{element:`Rhythm`,items:[`Music may move to a steady beat.`,`Music may move evenly or unevenly.`,`Music is made up of long sounds, short sounds and silences.`,`There are strong and weak beats in music.`,`Ta, ti-ti, and rest can show those sounds and silences.`]},{element:`Melody`,items:[`Sounds may be high or low.`,`A melody can move up, down, or stay the same.`,`Grade 1 sings so–mi and then so–mi–la, with hand signs.`]},{element:`Expression`,items:[`Music can be loud or quiet, fast or slow.`,`We start and stop together when a conductor’s hand asks us to.`]},{element:`Timbre`,items:[`A speaking voice and a singing voice sound different.`,`Classroom instruments and found sounds have their own colour.`]},{element:`Form`,items:[`Music can be the same or different.`,`Echo, call and response, and verse are forms Grade 1 can feel.`]},{element:`Skills`,items:[`Sing in unison, alone and with the group.`,`Move to the beat: walk, pat, freeze, hop as the body is ready.`,`Play a steady beat on rhythm instruments.`,`Listen with care, including music of First Nations, Métis, Inuit, and many cultures.`]}];export{o as a,c,l as d,i,u as l,m as n,f as o,a as r,d as s,p as t,s as u};